.\" originally written by Walter Harms
.Dd Aug 8, 2026
.\" Please adjust this date whenever revising the manpage.
.Dt SNTPD 8
.Sh NAME
.Nm sntpd
.Nd Simple NTP daemon and client
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl dhnrstv
.Op Fl i Ar SEC
.Op Fl m Ar SEC
.Op Fl p Ar PORT
.Op Fl q Ar USEC
.Op SERVER ...
.Nm ntpclient
.Op Fl dlrst
.Op Fl c Ar count
.Op Fl f Ar frequency
.Op Fl g Ar goodness
.Op Fl h Ar hostname
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl p Ar port
.Op Fl q Ar min_delay
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is an NTP (RFC-1305) client and server for UNIX-alike computers.  Its
functionality is a small subset of xntpd, but has the potential to
function better within that limited scope.  Since it is much smaller
than xntpd, it is also more relevant for embedded computers.  It
daemonizes itself by default and starts synchronizing with the given NTP
server, or pool.ntp.org if no server is given, using default settings.
.Pp
.Nm
has a very limited server mode, by default listening on UDP port 123 for
client connections.  The intention of this mode is to serve time from a
central router to small subnets where routing is not desired.  Similar
to a DHCP relay agent.
.Pp
.Nm
is a multicall binary.  When started as
.Nm ntpclient
it runs in a compatibility mode with the original by Larry Doolittle and
produces output similar to this:
.Bd -unfilled -offset indent
Day    Second     Elapsed   Stall  Skew      Dispersion  Freq
36765  00180.386  1398.0    40.3   953773.9  793.5       -1240000
36765  00780.382  1358.0    41.3   954329.0  915.5       -1240000
36765  01380.381  1439.0    56.0   954871.3  915.5       -1240000
.Ed
.Pp
The columns have the following meaning:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width DaysSeconds -compact -offset indent
.It Cm Day, Second
Time of measurement, UTC, relative to NTP epoch (Jan 1, 1900)
.It Cm Elapsed
Total time from query to response (microseconds)
.It Cm Stall
Time the server reports that it sat on the request (microseconds)
.It Cm Skew
Difference between local time and server time (microseconds)
.It Cm Dispersion
Reported by server, see RFC-1305 (microseconds)
.It Cm Freq
Local clock frequency adjustment (Linux only, ppm*65536)
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
The following options are supported by
.Nm
only.  See the
.Sx SYNOPSIS
section for available options for the
.Nm ntpclient
compat mode.  Note, if no
.Ar SERVER
argument is given,
.Nm
defaults to pool.ntp.org.  This is also the most prominent difference
to
.Nm ntpclient ,
which requires a server hostname to be given as argument.  See
.Sx SERVERS
below for the argument's syntax.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl d
Dry run, no time correction, useful for debugging.
.It Fl h
Show summary of command line options and exit.
.It Fl i Ar SEC
Check time every interval seconds, default 600.
.It Fl l Ar LEVEL
Set log level for syslog messages:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width WARNING -compact -offset indent
.It Cm none
Disable all logging
.It Cm error
Error conditions
.It Cm warning
Warning conditions
.It Cm notice
Normal but significant condition (default)
.It Cm info
Informational, all time sync events
.It Cm debug
Debug-level messages, like
.Cm info
but more, very noisy
.El
.It Fl m Ar SEC
Minimum interval between polls of the same server, default 15 seconds.
This is also the floor the failover backoff starts from.
.Pp
RFC 4330 requires that an SNTP client never poll faster than every 15
seconds, a rule meant to protect public servers such as the
.Ar pool.ntp.org
default.  Lowering it suits a closed network with its own time source,
where faster convergence costs nobody anything.
.Nm
logs a warning when it is set below 15.
.It Fl n
Don't fork.  Implies logging to stdout instead of syslog.  Add
.Fl s
to use syslog anyway, e.g. Finit + systemd.
.It Fl p Ar PORT
By default
.Nm
starts up its very limited server mode, prepared to serve clients with
time on UDP port 123.  This option can be used to change the server
listening port, or to disable the server mode, by setting port to zero
(0).
.It Fl q Ar USEC
Minimum packet delay for transaction, default 800 microseconds.
.It Fl r
Replay analysis code based on stdin, feature is disabled by default
at compile time.
.It Fl s
Use syslog instead of stdout for log messages, default unless started
with
.Fl n .
.It Fl t
Trust network and server, no RFC-4330 recommended cross-checks.  A
Kiss-o'-Death is the server's own request rather than one of those
checks, so it is exempt: see
.Sx SERVERS
below.
.It Fl v
Display
.Nm
version.
.El
.Sh SERVERS
One or more NTP servers may be given, each of the form
.Pp
.Dl host Ns Oo : Ns Ar port Oc Ns Oo , Ns Cm iburst Oc Ns Oo , Ns Cm prefer Oc
.Pp
repeated up to eight times.  IPv6 literals must be bracketed when a
port follows, e.g.
.Ar [2001:db8::1]:123 .
Omitting SERVER defaults to pool.ntp.org; giving one and leaving it
empty is a configuration error.
.Pp
.Nm
talks to one server at a time and treats the list as a failover
order, not a priority: it moves to the next entry only when the
current one stops answering, never on a timer, since every switch
resets the phase-lock loop.  A hostname that resolves to several
addresses contributes one candidate per address, tried in turn before
.Nm
moves on to the next server.  Because
.Xr getaddrinfo 3
does not promise the same order between resolves, this is best-effort:
each lap visits an address, not a guaranteed one.
.Nm
does not run the RFC 5905 selection and combining algorithms, so
several servers buy resilience rather than better accuracy.
.Pp
The number of addresses per name is not capped, so a name that stops
answering takes proportionally longer to give up on: every address gets
its own eight chances before
.Nm
moves to the next server in the list.  A name behind four addresses
therefore delays the move roughly fourfold.  The backoff bounds how
fast the probes go out, not how long the name takes to exhaust.
.Pp
There is no keyword for the RFC 7317
.Ar association-type
leaf.  RFC 5905 distinguishes a
.Cm server ,
one association, from a
.Cm pool ,
one association per resolved address polled at the same time and chosen
between.
.Nm
keeps a single association at any moment, so neither has anything to
attach to here: the addresses behind a name are places to fail over to,
not associations to maintain.  Symmetric-active
.Cm peer
mode is outside SNTP altogether.
.Bl -tag -width iburst -offset indent
.It Cm iburst
Send a burst of eight packets two seconds apart while the server is
unreachable, instead of one.  Speeds up the first sync and failover.
Off by default.
.It Cm prefer
Return to this server once it is reachable again, whatever its
position in the list.  Probed once per poll interval on its own
socket while
.Nm
is otherwise occupied; two consecutive valid replies are required
before switching, so a server answering only intermittently cannot
steal it back and forth.  Without this keyword none of that runs.
.El
.Pp
A stratum-0 reply is a Kiss-o'-Death, RFC 4330 section 8, and
.Nm
acts on the code the server gives: RATE widens that server's retry
backoff, and DENY or RSTR retire it for the rest of the run.  This
holds even under
.Fl t :
that flag waives the RFC 4330 section 5 checks
.Nm
otherwise insists on, but a Kiss-o'-Death is the server's own request
rather than one of those, so it is not waived.  If every server ends
up retired,
.Nm
logs the fact and exits with a non-zero status, so a supervisor can
tell that apart from a clean shutdown and back off before restarting.
